  "query_preview": "..\n\nGreater subsidence arrives this afternoon and has brought an end to \nthe main area of showers, but some isolated light rain/drizzle \nremain this afternoon. While showers largely come to an end, expect \ncloud cover to linger into the evening especially along and south of \nI-69. At least some low chances to see a stray shower along the \nsouthern border tonight as the stalled frontal boundary lingers \nacross northern IN/OH. Cooler northerly flow will be drawn into the \narea tonight by a surface high moving into Ontario. This will help \nbring morning overnight lows tonight down into the upper 40s across \nparts the far northern CWA in the Saginaw Valley and maybe parts of \nthe Thumb. \n\nThis surface high center will slide east into Quebec into tomorrow \nand be the influencing feature that will result dry conditions \ntomorrow. Southern extension of the thermal trough with surface flow \nout of the N-NE will bring daytime highs right around seasonal \nnormals in the mid 70s for most of southeast Michigan with around \n70s across the northern portions of the Thumb. \n\nA plume of deep moisture will then be drawn into Michigan late \nFriday and Saturday around the broad southeast CONUS ridge. Theta-e \nridge lifting into the region will contain PWATs of 1.50 inches to \n1.75 inches or greater. Increased flow under right entrance jet \ndynamics will help support larger scale synoptic lift northeast of a \ndeveloping trough across the plains. This will lead to an area of \nshowers and thunderstorms upstream across the western Great Lakes \nstates that will begin to move into southeast Michigan late tomorrow \nnight and across all of southeast Michigan Saturday. Likely showers \nblanket the majority of southeast Michigan by late Saturday morning \nwith a chance of thunderstorms mainly south of I-69. Instability is \nforecast to be rather weak keeping severe potential limited, but \nsome isolated embedded thunderstorms will be possible as bulk of \nactivity arrives during the day. Main concern for Saturday into \nSunday will be the heavy rainfall potential. Ensemble space still \nsupports a swath of 24 hour QPF of 0.75 to 1.5 inches Saturday 12Z \nto Sunday 12Z. Some of the recent hi-res guidance is allowing for a \nlittle bit of a break in heavier precip potential late afternoon \ninto early evening as lead wave of showers and possible \nthunderstorms weaken upon arrival before additional activity \nreinvigorates along the frontal zone Saturday evening into Sunday. \nThe swath of heaviest precipitation has shifted a slightly and is \nnow focused along and west of I-75 from Saginaw south to the greater \nDetroit metro region. A Slight Risk for Excessive Rainfall remains \nin place for much of southeast Michigan. Precipitation chances \ncontinue into Monday with greater uncertainty in precipitation \nchances into the middle portion of next week.",
